; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Fdc_Op6_PortA3_Off -- OPERATION 6: clear PA bit 3, then wait 5 ticks
; Called from: 3 site(s) in the module: Fdc_Op0_ResetAndIdentifyMedia, Fdc_Request__dispatch, Fdc_ResetAndIdentifyMedia  (Fdc_OperationJumpTable entry 6)
; Evidence: `ld A,(0x1E) / and A,0xF7 / ld (0x1E),A`; SFR 0x1E is PA
;          (tmp95c061.cpp:1363).  For unit 1 it calls 0xFE53E4 instead.
; Unknown:  what PA bit 3 is WIRED TO is still not established, but what the
;          firmware DOES with it now is -- see notes/FINDINGS-prom_a-gap-T-pa3.md
;          and `python3 notes/prom_a_pa3_census.py`.
;          ⚠ RETRACTED 2026-08-25, and it was wrong in a way worth recording:
;          this header used to say "operations 6 and 7 are its only writers, and
;          no other code in prom_a or prom_b touches it".  TWO MORE WRITERS EXIST
;          and always did -- 0xFE18EF `res 3,(0x1E)` and 0xFE18F7 `set 3,(0x1E)`,
;          in the block-device layer one level above this module, reached by 15
;          `calr` sites.  The scan behind the false claim looked for one encoding,
;          `f0 1e 41` = `ld (PA),A`; a bit-manipulation write is `f0 1e b3` /
;          `f0 1e bb` and is not that encoding.  A scan of ONE spelling cannot
;          support a claim about ALL writers.
;          What the two layers agree on: every write anywhere touches BIT 3 and no
;          other bit of PA, CLEARING is followed by a settling delay and SETTING is
;          not, and RESET leaves the bit HIGH (`ldio PA,0xF9` with `PACR = 0x0E`).
;          So the line is asserted LOW.  A drive-motor line fits every one of those
;          and is still NOT claimed here.
